terates on is:\r\r    f(vd) = vd*gr1 - Vin*gr2 - (id1 - id2) = 0\r    vd1 = -vd   (D1: anode at node, cathode at Vout)\r    vd2 = +vd   (D2: cathode at node, anode at Vout)\r\rwhich is exactly the fixed point of the original update formula\r    vsol = ( Vin*(gr1 + gr2 + gd1 + gd2) + id1eq - id2eq ) / (gr1 + gd1 + gd2)\ri.e. vsol*gr1 = Vin*(gr1 + gr2) + id1 - id2. This version solves that same\requation, but for vd directly instead of iterating on Vout, for the\rnumerical reasons below.\r\rNUMERICS (warm-started, bracketed Newton on the diode voltage)\r--------------------------------------------------------------\r* Solve for vd = Vout - Vin instead of Vout. vd is physically bounded to\r  about +-0.8 V for any current the resistors can supply (vd = Vt*ln(id/Is)),\r  so the seed, the iterate box and the solution bracket are all +-VD_CLAMP.\r* Bracketed Newton: f(vd) is strictly monotone increasing (df/dvd =\r  gr1 + gd1 + gd2 > 0), so the root is bracketed by f(-VD_CLAMP) < 0 <\r  f(VD_CLAMP). Each iteration shrinks the bracket with the current point's\r  sign, then takes the Newton step only when it is safe (aggressive: at\r  least half a bracket-width away, or residual already small);  otherwise it\r  takes the bracket midpoint. Midpoints halve the bracket and accepted\r  Newton steps only occur where they are exact (linear region) or already\r  near the root, so in practice the solve stays within ~15 iterations no\r  matter how bad the seed. This is what makes it robust where a bare Newton\r  loop is not: a raw Newton step from a cold/far seed can jump to ~2*Vin\r  (deep into the exponential, where Newton then crawls at ~Vt per iteration\r  and never finishes in budget), and at high gain the diode voltage swings\r  as gain*Vin through the linear region, which sign-flips the seed.\r* Warm start: the previous sample's diode voltage is kept in a History\r  variable and used as the next sample's seed. vd changes far more slowly\r  than the signal itself (it is ~0.7 V whether the input is quiet or loud,\r  and only tracks Vin logarithmically), so the seed is typically within a\r  few millivolts of the new solution and the loop finishes in 1-3\r  iterations instead of the fixed 20 the original burned on every sample.\r  Because the nonlinearity runs at 4x the base rate, the four branch calls\r  share this History in time order, giving exactly time-adjacent seeds.\r* VD_CLAMP = 1.2 V is comfortably above the true solution (reaching it\r  needs ~kA of diode current) and keeps every exp() argument below\r  EXP_CLAMP*VT (~2.21 V), so the exponential is never clamped while Newton\r  is solving -- the clamp box can never create the spurious fixed points\r  described in diode_clip.txt.\r* The exp() argument is additionally clamped to +-EXP_CLAMP as a last line\r  of defense: float32 exp() overflows to inf at |arg| ~= 88, and inf/inf\r  gives NaN, which would permanently poison the History state (a NaN in a\r  gen history never recovers). With the diode-voltage clamp above this is\r  never active -- it is belt-and-braces. (The original had no clamps at all:\r  on the first iteration v_out = 0 so vd1 = v_in, and any |v_in| > ~2.3 V\r  made exp() overflow and the whole sample come out NaN.)\r* If the warm-started solve fails to converge, a cold restart from vd = 0\r  is attempted (also clamped to +-VD_CLAMP).\r* gain is clamped to [0.01, 100] so gr1 = 1/(gain*10000) can never become\r  infinite (gain = 0 would divide by zero).\r\rPARAMETER PLACEMENT\r-------------------\rgenexpr requires all function definitions to precede every statement:\r  circuit parameters (IS, VT)        -> declared in DiodeDistortion, passed\r                                        into diodeSolve as arguments\r  conductances (gr1, gr2)            -> computed in DiodeDistortion from the\r                                        gain, passed into diodeSolve\r  solver parameters (MAX_ITER, TOL,\r                     F_TOL, VD_CLAMP, EXP_CLAMP) -> declared inside diodeSolve\r  derived value (invVt)              -> computed inside diodeSolve\r\rPATCH I/O\r---------\r  in1  audio input\r  in2  gain (clipped to [0.01, 100]) -> scales R1; sets the clip knee\r  out1 processed audio, DC-blocked\r\rThe audio is oversampled 4x with polyphase-IIR halfband filters so the\rclipping harmonics alias less, then downsampled and DC-blocked.\r============================================================================\r*/\r\rrequire(\"halfband_ppiir.genexpr\");\r// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\r// One solve of the diode network for a fixed v_in, starting from a seed for\r// the diode voltage vd = Vout - Vin (usually the previous sample's converged\r// vd, passed in as seed_offset). Circuit values (is, vt) and conductances\r// (gr1, gr2) are owned by DiodeDistortion and passed in; solver parameters\r// are declared here. Returns the solution vsol and whether it converged.\r// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\rdiodeSolve(v_in, seed_offset, gr1, gr2, is, vt)\r{\r    // ---- solver parameters ----\r    MAX_ITER = 20;\r    VD_CLAMP = 1.2;\r    EXP_CLAMP = 85.0;\r\r    invVt = 1.0 / vt;\r\r    vd = clip(seed_offset, -VD_CLAMP, VD_CLAMP);\r    lo = -VD_CLAMP;\r    hi = VD_CLAMP;\r\r    ed1 = exp(clip(-vd * invVt, -EXP_CLAMP, EXP_CLAMP));\r    id1 = is * ed1 - is;\r    gd1 = is * ed1 * invVt;\r\r    ed2 = exp(clip(vd * invVt, -EXP_CLAMP, EXP_CLAMP));\r    id2 = is * ed2 - is;\r    gd2 = is * ed2 * invVt;\r\r    f = vd * gr1 - v_in * gr2 - (id1 - id2);\r\r    for (i = 0; i < MAX_ITER; i += 1) {\r\r        if (f > 0) { hi = vd; } else { lo = vd; }\r\r        vd_new = vd;\r\r        vd_newton = vd - f / (gr1 + gd1 + gd2);\r        take_newton = 0;\r        if (vd_newton > lo && vd_newton < hi) {\r            if (abs(f) < 0.001) { take_newton = 1; }\r            else if (abs(vd_newton - vd) >= (hi - lo) * 0.5) { take_newton = 1; }\r        }\r        if (take_newton == 1) {\r            vd_new = vd_newton;\r        } else {\r            vd_new = (lo + hi) * 0.5;\r        }\r\r        ed1 = exp(clip(-vd_new * invVt, -EXP_CLAMP, EXP_CLAMP));\r        id1 = is * ed1 - is;\r        gd1 = is * ed1 * invVt;\r\r        ed2 = exp(clip(vd_new * invVt, -EXP_CLAMP, EXP_CLAMP));\r        id2 = is * ed2 - is;\r        gd2 = is * ed2 * invVt;\r\r        vd = vd_new;\r        f = vd * gr1 - v_in * gr2 - (id1 - id2);\r    }\r\r    return v_in + vd, 1;\r}\r\r// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\r// Per-sample diode overdrive. v_out is a History variable holding the\r// previous sample's converged solution; vd_prev holds the previous sample's\r// diode voltage, used to warm-start the next solve. DiodeDistortion is\r// called on up0..up3 in time order, so its shared History state warm-starts\r// each branch from the previous branch's solution -- exactly the right\r// time-adjacent seed.\r// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\rDiodeDistortion (v_in, gain) {\r\r    History v_out(0);\r    History vd_prev(0);\r\r    // ---- circuit parameters (owned by the circuit stage) ----\r    is = 1E-15;              // diode saturation current [A]\r    vt = 26E-3;              // thermal voltage [V] (kT/q at ~25 C)\r    gr1 = 1.0/(gain * 10000);  // input-side conductance [S], gain-scaled\r    gr2 = 1.0/10000;           // feedback conductance [S]\r\r    // warm-started solve (diodeSolve clamps the seed internally)\r    v_out, converged = diodeSolve(v_in, vd_prev, gr1, gr2, is, vt);\r\r    // fallback: cold restart from vd = 0 if the warm start misbehaved.\r    // (Best-effort: v_out is still clamped and finite.)\r    if (!converged) {\r        v_out, converged = diodeSolve(v_in, 0, gr1, gr2, is, vt);\r    }\r\r    vd_prev = v_out - v_in;   // remember this sample's diode voltage\r\r    return v_out;\r}\r\r// ---- main signal path ----\r\rgain = clip(in2, 0.01, 100);   // keep gr1 finite and the solve well-posed\r\rup0, up1, up2, up3 = upsample4x_ppiir(in1);\r\rup0 = DiodeDistortion(up0, gain);\rup1 = DiodeDistortion(up1, gain);\rup2 = DiodeDistortion(up2, gain);\rup3 = DiodeDistortion(up3, gain);\r\rout1 = dcblock(downsample4x_ppiir(up0, up1, up2, up3));\r"
                                                    }
                                                },
